## 3. Local UI development

### Prerequisites (one-time)

```bash
# From repo root — install per-app deps as needed
cd apps/customer-web && npm ci    # or admin-web / vendor-web
cd backend/lambda && npm ci       # only if working on API locally
```

Ensure **AWS CLI** is configured (`aws sts get-caller-identity`) when pointing at dev RDS or deploying.

### Option A — UI against **dev API** (most common)

Hot reload; no local backend required. Uses UAT OTP `123456` when `NEXT_PUBLIC_UAT_MODE=true`.

```bash
# From repo root (Windows cmd-style env vars shown; use export on bash/WSL)
npm run dev:customer    # http://localhost:3001
npm run dev:vendor      # http://localhost:3002
npm run dev:admin       # http://localhost:3000 (approx — check terminal output)
```

Dev API: `https://z0b3obweb6.execute-api.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com`

### Option B — UI against **local backend**

Terminal 1 — API:

```bash
cd backend/lambda
cp .env.local.example .env.local   # if present; otherwise create .env.local
# Set UAT_MODE=true, DB_* if using local Postgres or dev RDS read-only
npm run start:local                # default http://localhost:3000
```

Terminal 2 — frontend:

```bash
npm run local:customer   # or local:vendor / local:admin
```

### Option C — UI against **prod API** (read-only investigation only)

```bash
npm run prod:customer    # or prod:vendor / prod:admin
```

**Guardrail:** Use prod API locally only for debugging. **Never** test writes, migrations, or destructive actions against prod from local.

### UI change discipline

- Match existing patterns in the app you edit (components, hooks, Tailwind, API client).
- Keep diffs minimal — no drive-by refactors.
- After UI changes, confirm the page loads and the changed flow works in the browser.
- Do not add markdown docs unless the user asks.

